---
title: Images
summary: Image fields with alt text, focal points and crops, what happens to an upload before it's saved, and how to render the result.
---

## Add an image field

Use `fields.image()` and pass it Astro's `image` helper from the schema function, so `src` still goes through Astro's image pipeline:

```ts
import { defineCollection } from 'astro:content';
import { glob } from 'astro/loaders';
import { z } from 'astro/zod';
import { fields } from 'vespercms/fields';

const blog = defineCollection({
  loader: glob({ pattern: '**/[^_]*.md', base: './src/content/blog' }),
  schema: ({ image }) =>
    z.object({
      title: z.string(),
      hero: fields.image(image, { ratios: ['wide', 'square'] }).optional(),
    }),
});
```

`ratios` names the crops the studio previews for this field. Without it, the studio previews every ratio the site declares.

A plain `image()` field works too, but it stores only a path: no alt text, focal point or crops.

## What the file stores

```yaml
hero:
  src: ../../assets/blog/harbor-at-dawn.jpg
  alt: An orange sun low over a dark harbor, with one small boat heading out
  focus: { x: 0.62, y: 0.35 }
  crops:
    square: { x: 0.40, y: 0.10, w: 0.45, h: 0.675 }
```

- `src` is relative to the entry's file, as Astro's `image()` expects.
- `alt` is required unless the image is decorative, which stores `alt: ""` and `decorative: true`. The schema checks this, so a save without either is refused: "Describe this image, or mark it decorative."
- `focus` is the focal point and `crops` holds a rectangle per ratio. Every number is a share of the image, 0 to 1 from the top left.

## In the studio

The image field shows the picture with "Replace image" and "Remove image", then the alt text: "Describe what this image communicates on this page." Accessibility notes beside the box point out alt text that's a file name, starts with "Image of", or runs too long: see [Accessibility](/docs/site/accessibility/). The "Decorative image" checkbox is for pictures that add nothing a reader needs.

"Focal point & crops" opens below it:

- **Focal point.** Click or tap the image to set it, or drag the marker. With focus on the image, the arrow keys move it 1%, or 10% with Shift. "Across (%)" and "Down (%)" set it by number.
- **Crops.** There's a preview for each ratio, such as "Wide · 16:9", cut around the focal point. "Adjust crop" opens a box fixed to that ratio: drag it or a corner, or use the arrow keys, with Shift to resize. Enter applies and Escape cancels. The preview then says "Custom crop", and "Reset to focal point" goes back.

These edits change only the entry. The image file itself is never rewritten.

Replacing the image clears its focal point and crops, which were chosen for the old picture, and keeps its alt text, with a note to check that the words still describe the new one.

## Ratios

Declare the site's ratios once, in `vesper.config.ts`, as width divided by height:

```ts
// vesper.config.ts
images: {
  ratios: { wide: 16 / 9, square: 1, portrait: 4 / 5 },
  maxUploadEdge: 3000,
},
```

The studio previews these, and `<Img ratio="wide">` renders the same crop, so editors see what visitors get. `og` (1.91:1, for social images) is built in unless you declare your own. A field that lists a ratio the site doesn't declare gets a warning.

## Uploads

An image uploaded from a field goes into the folder of the image it replaces, otherwise `src/assets/<collection>/`. A file field (`fields.file()`) uploads to `public/files/` and stores `/files/name.pdf`, because a file people download has to be served as it is. The Media screen, in the Site menu, lists everything in `src/assets` and `public` with the pages that use it.

In GitHub mode, an upload from a field is published with the entry, in one commit.

Before anything is saved, the studio cleans each upload in the browser:

- **Location and camera data** (EXIF, XMP, IPTC) are removed from JPEG, PNG and WebP files. When the pixels don't change, the image data stays byte for byte the original, colour profile included.
- **Sideways photos** are turned upright, from their EXIF orientation.
- **Large photos** are scaled down so the longer edge is at most `maxUploadEdge` pixels (3000 by default). Turning or scaling a photo means encoding it again, which drops all its metadata.
- **iPhone photos (HEIC)** become JPEG where the browser can decode them, which today means Safari. Elsewhere the studio asks you to export a JPEG first, or to upload from Safari.
- **SVG files** lose scripts, event handlers, `javascript:` and `data:` links, and embedded HTML.
- **Duplicates** are found by content. If the file is already in the site, you choose between "Use the existing file" and "Upload a copy anyway".
- **Size.** A file over 5 MB asks first, and over 20 MB is refused.

Image fields accept JPEG, PNG, WebP, AVIF, GIF and SVG, checked by the file's contents, not its name.

## Rendering on your site

`<Img>` renders an image field through Astro's own `<Image>`, `<Picture>` and `getImage()`:

```astro
---
import { Img } from 'vespercms/components';
const { post } = Astro.props;
---
<Img image={post.data.hero} ratio="wide" widths={[480, 960, 1440]} sizes="100vw" />
<Img image={post.data.hero} art={{ base: 'square', '(min-width: 48rem)': 'wide' }} />
<Img image={post.data.hero} layout="full-width" />
```

| Props | What you get |
|---|---|
| `ratio` | A ratio's name from `images.ratios`, or a number. The editor's crop for that ratio if there is one, otherwise the crop around the focal point. |
| `art` | A `<picture>` with a source per media query, each with its own crop, and `base` for everything else. `formats` and `fallbackFormat` work as in Astro's `<Picture>`. |
| neither | The whole image, fluid. The focal point becomes `object-position`, so CSS cropping keeps the subject in frame. |

Alt text always comes from the field, and a decorative image gets `alt=""`. Every other Astro image prop passes through, and `srcset` never offers a width larger than the crop.

A picture can be content on its own page and only repeat the words beside it somewhere else, such as a cover inside a card whose title follows. There, pass `decorative` for `alt=""`, whatever the field says:

```astro
<a href={getUrl(post)}>
  <Img image={post.data.hero} ratio="wide" decorative />
  <h3>{post.data.title}</h3>
</a>
```

`<Img>` and `getImg()` don't take `alt`: the build stops with a message saying to pass `decorative` instead, so the editor's words are never overridden by a template's.

## Social images

For places an `<img>` can't go, such as `og:image`, `getImg()` returns Astro's `getImage()` result for the same crop:

```astro
---
import { getImg } from 'vespercms/components';
const og = await getImg({ image: post.data.hero, ratio: 'og', width: 1200 });
---
<meta property="og:image" content={new URL(og.src, Astro.site)} />
```

## How crops are cut

With Astro's default Sharp image service, Vesper adds a step to it: the stored rectangle is cut from the upright image, then Astro resizes and encodes it as usual. The crops are ordinary files in `dist/`, so they work on any host. The build log says the step is on. It uses the copy of `sharp` that Astro's own service loads.

`vesper({ imageService: false })` turns the step off. Then, and with a host's image service (Cloudflare, Netlify, Vercel), `<Img>` asks Astro for the ratio's size, which is Astro's own server crop, and keeps the focal point as `object-position`. Crops an editor adjusted by hand are applied only with the step on.

{% callout type="warning" title="Keep focal points out of position" %}
Never pass a focal point to Astro's `position` prop. Sharp accepts only its own keywords there, such as `top` or `left`, and a value like `62% 35%` fails the build. `<Img>` writes the focal point into `style` as `object-position`. Do the same if you render an image field with Astro's components directly.
{% /callout %}
